{
  "trace_id": "hybrid::q14",
  "architecture": "hybrid",
  "question": "A needle-in-a-haystack test can score perfectly on recall@k while the assembled answer is still wrong \u2014 what's the mechanism behind that gap, and where in the pipeline does the actual fix belong?",
  "answer": "A needle\u2011in\u2011the\u2011haystack evaluation can report 100\u202f%\u202fRecall@k because the **right chunk is indeed present among the top\u202fk retrieved pieces** \u2013 Recall@k only asks \u201cis the correct evidence anywhere in the set?\u201d and does not care *where* it ends up in the list\u202f\u3010retrieval-metrics-recall-at-k-and-mrr::0\u3011.  \n\nWhen the retrieved chunks are then assembled into a prompt, the model may still produce a wrong answer if the correct chunk is placed in an unfavourable position (e.g., in the middle of a long context). This \u201clost\u2011in\u2011the\u2011middle\u201d effect means the information is technically available but the model fails to attend to it, so the answer is incorrect even though retrieval succeeded\u202f\u3010retrieval-failures-lost-in-the-middle::0\u3011.  \n\nThe fix therefore does **not** belong in the retriever itself but in the **prompt\u2011template / ordering stage**: rank chunks by relevance (or confidence) and deliberately put the highest\u2011confidence chunk near the start or end of the context so the model can use it reliably\u202f\u3010retrieval-failures-lost-in-the-middle::1\u3011. In short, the gap arises from a downstream \u201cposition\u2011bias\u201d failure, and the remedy is to adjust how retrieved evidence is ordered before it is fed to the model.",
